From c609c5d867856e1a090d2f6416eee5dec2888cdb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: amd-weisun <Wei.Sun@amd.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 12:49:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Serve the BF16 grouped GEMM forward and dgrad ops from aiter
 on ROCm where it is faster

Route mslk::bf16bf16bf16_grouped_stacked and mslk::bf16bf16bf16_grouped_grad
through the shared per-shape routing (#573), as f4f4bf16 does (#574), with
aiter's Triton gmm as the second backend. gmm takes both ops' arguments as
free views, and on every shape checked its outputs were bitwise identical to
MSLK's kernels'.

- Adapters (mslk/gemm/aiter/): stacked passes W [G, N, K] as
  W.transpose(1, 2), gmm's unambiguous transposed layout (as is, gmm rejects
  K == N); dgrad passes W.permute(0, 2, 1), gmm's non-transposed layout.
  num_sms becomes grid_dim.
- Routing (mslk/gemm/dispatch/): key (G, N, K), bucketed on total rows M.
  aiter takes non-empty BF16 calls with the expected strides and an exactly
  sized output, and for dgrad K != N; other calls use MSLK's kernel.
- Tuning scripts, shape lists (MoE layers of public models; uneven group
  sizes) and tuned tables for gfx950. aiter serves 17 of 176 stacked rows
  (1.04x-1.30x) and 20 of 170 dgrad rows (1.06x-1.40x), all at M >= 2048.
- Tests: both ops' unit tests run under the routing and with each backend
  forced, including shapes routed to aiter and K == N; dispatch_test.py and
  lazy_import_test.py cover the new modules.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
